    (v_case_id, 'Which of the following benzodiazepines is preferred in older adults because it bypasses Phase I CYP450 oxidation and undergoes direct Phase II conjugation, avoiding toxic accumulation?', '["Chlordiazepoxide", "Diazepam", "Flurazepam", "Lorazepam", "Clonazepam"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The preferred benzodiazepines in the elderly are Lorazepam, Oxazepam, and Temazepam (the ''LOT'' drugs) because they bypass Phase I hepatic oxidation (which declines with age) and undergo direct Phase II conjugation (glucuronidation), which remains preserved.'),
    (v_case_id, 'Which of the following clinical tools is most appropriate for a pharmacist or physician to consult when evaluating a multi-drug regimen for potentially inappropriate medications in an elderly patient?', '["AGS Beers Criteria", "Gage Score", "Mini Nutritional Assessment", "HAS-BLED Score", "Braden Scale Tool"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The AGS Beers Criteria is the gold-standard reference list of medications that should be avoided or used with caution in older adults due to high risk of adverse drug reactions, including falls and cognitive decline.');

