    (v_case_id, 'What is the best pharmacological explanation for this patient''s complete lack of pain relief?', '["Codeine is an active drug that is rapidly inactivated by CYP2D6; poor metabolizers accumulate codeine, which causes a paradoxical pain sensitivity", "The patient''s opioid receptors have a primary genetic mutation that prevents all narcotic binding", "Codeine is a prodrug that must be converted to its active metabolite (morphine) by the hepatic enzyme CYP2C9; poor metabolizers cannot perform this conversion, resulting in therapeutic failure", "None of the options; poor metabolizers have no response to any pain medications", "The patient''s codeine was rapidly excreted by upregulated renal organic cation transporters"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Codeine is a prodrug with weak analgesic activity. It must be converted to **morphine** (which has high mu-opioid receptor affinity) by the hepatic enzyme **CYP2D6**. In CYP2D6 ''poor metabolizers'' (about 5-10% of Caucasians), this conversion is absent, leading to a complete lack of pain relief. Conversely, ''ultra-rapid metabolizers'' convert too much codeine, risking morphine toxicity.'),
    (v_case_id, 'Which of the following other common prodrug opioids requires hepatic conversion to its active metabolite by CYP2D6 to provide effective analgesia?', '["Methadone", "Morphine", "Buprenorphine", "Tramadol", "Fentanyl"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Tramadol, like codeine, is a prodrug that must be converted to its active O-desmethyltramadol (M1) metabolite by CYP2D6 to provide meaningful mu-opioid receptor analgesia. Poor metabolizers will also fail to respond to tramadol.'),
    (v_case_id, 'In a patient who is identified as a CYP2D6 ''ultra-rapid metabolizer,'' what represents the primary clinical danger if they are prescribed standard doses of codeine?', '["Life-threatening morphine toxicity (respiratory depression and coma) at standard therapeutic doses", "No danger; ultra-rapid metabolizers simply clear the drug faster without side effects", "Severe hepatorenal syndrome due to drug precipitation in the gallbladder", "Complete lack of drug absorption leading to severe therapeutic failure", "Rapid development of a severe allergic rash (Stevens-Johnson syndrome)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'CYP2D6 ultra-rapid metabolizers convert codeine to morphine exceptionally quickly. This leads to rapid accumulation of morphine, putting the patient at risk of severe opioid toxicity, including respiratory depression and death, even at normal, prescribed doses.');

