    (v_case_id, 'Which of the following other common prodrug opioids requires hepatic conversion to its active metabolite by CYP2D6 to provide effective analgesia?', '["Morphine", "Tramadol", "Fentanyl", "Buprenorphine", "Methadone"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Tramadol, like codeine, is a prodrug that must be converted to its active O-desmethyltramadol (M1) metabolite by CYP2D6 to provide meaningful mu-opioid receptor analgesia. Poor metabolizers will also fail to respond to tramadol.'),
    (v_case_id, 'In a patient who is identified as a CYP2D6 ''ultra-rapid metabolizer,'' what represents the primary clinical danger if they are prescribed standard doses of codeine?', '["Rapid development of a severe allergic rash (Stevens-Johnson syndrome)", "Life-threatening morphine toxicity (respiratory depression and coma) at standard therapeutic doses", "Severe hepatorenal syndrome due to drug precipitation in the gallbladder", "Complete lack of drug absorption leading to severe therapeutic failure", "No danger; ultra-rapid metabolizers simply clear the drug faster without side effects"]'::jsonb, 1, 'CYP2D6 ultra-rapid metabolizers convert codeine to morphine exceptionally quickly. This leads to rapid accumulation of morphine, putting the patient at risk of severe opioid toxicity, including respiratory depression and death, even at normal, prescribed doses.');

